        <Document ID="6A3AB75E-99E0-4B44-BCD8-F510E3A06123">
            <Title>Scene-1</Title>
            <Text>Falling to my death is not the way I thought my life would end. But here I am falling face first to the earth with no parachute.  As I get closer to my final resting place I start to recognize the scene below. I’m over the River. The thick cypress trees and crescent shaped rock formation signaled a familiar place. I don’t want to die here. Not here. 
Ask and you shall receive I guess. In a second I realized my swift plunge had slowed. 
I am floating like a dandelion on the  breeze until I end up hovering over the water. Theres something moving just below the surface. Make that someone.

The details of the person’s face were hard to see through the water.  Long dark hair floated around like a graceful shadow as she looked all around. The woman’s outstretched arms frantically searched for something.  A way out?  A hand stretched in my direction and I realized that she was reaching for me. 
I’m terrified but I can’t just leave her to a watery grave.  I stretched my arm out toward her until our fingertips met. The cloudy water started to clear just as my fingers broke the surface. Then as if I have tripped an alarm an loud beeping starts.
Autumn gasped and coughed she sat up in bed. She was in her bed. In her room. Safe. The digital clock on her dresser read 8:16. 
“At least I didn’t oversleep today”
Knock Knock Knock
“Are you up?”
Autumn realized she was grasping the ring that hung around her neck. Something she often did when she was scared or uneasy.
“Uh Um Yeah. I’ll be out in a minute.”
“Are you ok?” “You sound strange”
Autumn took a deep calming breath before replying. Violet was a great room mate and even better friend. But sometime it was had to believe they were the same age violet was almost as overprotective as a Father on prom night. 
“Vi I’m fine. Not everyone can be like you in the mornings Susie Sunshine.”
“Fine” Violet laughed But muffins are fresh out of the oven and I need a taste tester.”
“Mmmm.”” Ok but give me a few minutes ready for work first.” Said Autumn  “I’m like 80 percent sure Allen is gonna fire me if I’m late again. Autumn responded
I hopped out of bed and headed for the bathroom down the hall.
Violet was attempting to perfect her cranberry orange flavored muffins this week. And since Violet was an amazing cook Autumn had no problem being the taste tester.
Twenty minutes later Autumn sat across the table from her best friend. The house that they shared was small but did include a breakfast nook with a bay window overlooking the front yard. 
Hows you head? Vi asked 
Oh much better. 
Autumn had over heated about two weeks ago and passed out in the front yard. Thankfully Violet had left work early and found her near the front steps. When Autumn woke up someone was playing a cymbal inside her head and shining a police search light in her eyes. Autumn had hit her head when she fell. Vi insisted on taking her to the hospital but that was a no go. Autumn would rather crawl over hot coals than go to a hospital. The compromise was Violet making a terrible herbal remedy. It smelled like sweaty onions and old potatoes but it did the trick. Autumn felt great. The only lingering affect was these strange dreams but she didn’t dare mention them to Vi for fear of another herbal fix.
I better get going Autumn said rising from her seat. She tilted the cup back to finish her orange juice before sitting it in the sink.


Good thing muffins were great for breakfast on the go. Autumn took another bite as she walked out the door.  The roses she and Vi had planted near the front door were looking great. A few red buds where already showing life. Autumn had alway seemed to have a green thumb. Even as a child planting things that could grow and produce something beautiful had fascinated her.  As an adult Autumn kind of liked to think of herself as a flower that could produce beauty in her artwork. Artwork that one day would be appreciated. 

 But for now Autumn hopped in her blue Honda hatchback and headed off to work.  The homemade breakfast confection perfumed the cabin. The smell of oranges always made her feel happy. But anything that Violet cooked had that effect. 

It happened so suddenly. Just as she started to cross Webb Creek there was a loud Bang and the steering wheel jerked to the right. On a reaction Autumn pulled to left. Over correcting made things worse. Thank goodness she didn't end up in the creek. The guard rail stopped the Honda’s forward progress. But the sudden stopped caused her head to bang the driver side window and then everything went black.



The smell of disinfectant and hand sanitizer filled her nose as she tried to gain her bearings.  Looking around the room, Autumn clutched the silver ring that alway hung from the gold chain around her neck It took a few blinks to focus in the dimly lit room. 
A hospital?
Why…
Suddenly Autumn’s sluggish mind coughed up the events of the day. It was like a slide show was playing in her mind. She remembered donning her U-Store It t-shirt, grabbing her backpack and a muffin and heading out the door.

Autumn! I’m so glad you’re ok. Violet said as she rushed to the side of the hospital bed. 
Violet sat on the bed and quickly wrapped her arms around Autumn. 
Oh Autumn I was so worried. Doug called and said you never made it to work. I’m so sorry that this happened to you. 
I’m alright. 
I think I’m alright. No broken bones at least. Besides if was an accident.  I think my tire blew.  I guess I waited too long to replace them. I’m just glad no one else was hurt.
So am I said Violet.
How long have I been here?
You left for work at 8:30 and the hospital called me around 10.
What time is it now?
Just after 5:00.
You’ve been here all day?

Of course. Thats what family is for.
She and Violet were not related by blood but had formed a bond as close as sister.  Autumn had been looking for a new roommate and Violet found one of her flyers.
Talk about bad luck. Autumn had lost her keys, nearly set fire to the breakroom at work. Now this car accident. 
In walked a tall thin man in a long white coat.
Good afternoon Miss North. I’m Dr. Moore. I am glad to see you’re up and alert.
Thanks. So when can I go home?
We need to observe you overnight due to the head injury.
Autumn reached for the injury near her hairline and winced.
I feel fine Doc.
25 years old with no pre existing health conditions. Im sure you’ll make a full recovery. But hospital policy for a head injury and loss of consciousness is an overnight stay.  ke
Now I need to check out a few other things now that you are awake.
Thank you for being so thorough Doctor Said Violet
Yeah thanks.
You are very welcome Miss North. 

      *** 

Jack gazed out over the hill at the sun rise. He knew the view extremely well. The same canopy of  trees. The same rushing stream that trickled it’s way down the mountain. This was always the reward for his morning run. Hell just for getting out of bed before dawn. This view reminded him of why he worked so hard. But today he just needed time to think away from the rest of the guys.  Peace and quite.
“Jack”
“Hey Jack”
Well there went his quite.
“Fuck. I don’t need a run partner or a baby sitter, Carver”
 “I know you asked for space but the queen has requested an audience.” Carver replied
“When?” Jack asked 
“Now.”
“Alright. Has the Squad been informed?
“No. Said Carver. You don’t understand. The queen requested a private audience with you.”
“Shit.” Jack said through clenched teeth
And there went he peace.

The queen had asked for a private meeting with him. She probably had finally gotten sick of his shit. Getting black out intoxicated wasn’t such a great idea. Especially when your whole reason for being was to stay alert and protect those in your care. Unfortunately losing someone in your care made it fucking hard to sleep. With sleep came the nightmares. The replay of that night that Jack would never forget.
As they arrived at the Palace courtyard both Carver and Jack headed to the South lawn.  The palace stood on the north lawn. It had been built over 100 years ago to house the rulers of the race. 
On the south lawn just behind a grove of trees stood a two story carriage house in habited by The Squad.
The Squad was a group of male Fae who protected their race. A race that was not as close nit at they used to be.  Some Fae were not even aware of there magical heritage now. The Squad could trace their lines back for over one hundred years.  Unfortunately you couldnt choose your genetic lineage and the bloodlines of the Squad had been tainted.  Each and every male of the Squad had pledged to lay down thier lives for their queen without question or hesitation. Queen Rayvn had reluctantly ascended to the thrown after her mother was killed in the attack that started the war.

After a quick shower, Jack headed to meet with Queen Rayvn. The queen was waiting in the oversized chair near a window.  As always she seemed a bit uncomfortable.  As if she didnt belong.  

“Hello my queen”
“Hi. Its very nice to see you this morning Jack” the queen replied 
Jack fell silent not knowing what to say next. The queen broke the silence.
“ I asked for you to come here because I need your help.”
“Of course my queen” Anything that you request is law”
The queen visibly flinched at this.
I apologize Queen Ravyn. I did not mean to -
Please. There is no need for you to apologize Jack. Really.  As I said I need your help with something. I need you to retrieve a wayward Fae. 
She lives in the Hill Country as a human. This Fae is unaware of her heritage. Her name is Autumn and she…
The queen took a breath and turn away to gaze out of the window. The sun shone in around her creating an auburn halo around her coils of dark hair. Funny how Jack had never noticed this before. He had alway assumed that the queen had been named for her dark black hair but in reality her hair was a deep dark red. 
After a moment Queen Ravyn continued. She needs our protection. The queen grabbed an inconspicuous piece of paper from the window sill. I was visited by the elder Vervina about three weeks ago. She had a vision of this young Fae. The queen said revealing a printed black and white photo of a young Fae woman. She was kneeling next to a small boy with a toy truck in her hand. A fire truck. The boy was beaming as he admired what was obviously a new toy. And though only the Fae’s profile was visible, Jack could see that she was beaming right back at the little boy. For a moment Jack felt his heat fall out of rhythm.  Probably from the beautiful display of caring. Jack had seen far to little of that type of caring in his life.
Forgive me Queen Ravyn, Jack said but are you positive that I am the one that you wish to send. I don’t know a lot about the young ones.
Young ones?
Oh. The young boy in the picture is not child. Autumn volunteers at an orphanage called Rainbow Valley in the Hill Country. When Vervina visited me with news of her vision I asked her to draw a picture of the wayward Fae. Once we knew what she looked like were able to find her with the facial recognition software. This photo is from a news article about a Holiday toy drive at the Rainbow Valley orphanage. 
Jack eye the photo again and again his heart skipped a beat.  
May I ask my queen, what vision of this Fae did Vervina see?
Vervina told me that the Dark Storm is upon us. And that Autumn is the only Fae with enough light to save the Fae race.






</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="7BB69EF8-2678-4174-96B1-EB6D7CD7FB2B"/>
        <Document ID="8011470C-3DAB-46BE-A166-FCF536FE86F6">
            <Title>Standard Manuscript</Title>
            <Text>Andrew Nonymous                   700 words.
Your manuscript should
have your address
and contact details
on the title page only.

If you have an agent, your
agent's address can be
included beneath.

NOVEL STANDARD MANUSCRIPT FORMAT
            by Andrew Nonymous


                                                            Nonymous / MANUSCRIPT FORMAT / 1

                                                 Chapter One

         Standard manuscript format is often required for novels so
that work can easily be read by editors. It allows editors to
estimate word count and determine roughly how many pages will be
required to produce a book using the page size, style and font of
their house. This PDF file was generated via Scrivener's compile
feature and was created from the &quot;Novel&quot; project template.

         Manuscripts for full-length novels require a separate title
page. The author's name, address, telephone number and e-mail
address should be shown in the upper-left corner of the title
page, single-spaced. The title of the story should appear in all
caps about halfway down the page. The author's name follows the
title on the by-line as the author wants it to appear when
published. A real name or pseudonym may be used and may include
initials, actual given name or professional designation.

         All text should be double-spaced and left-justified with a
ragged right margin. Paragraphs should be indented by about five


                                                            Nonymous / MANUSCRIPT FORMAT / 2

spaces (half an inch) and not separated by an additional blank
line. (It used to be traditional to separate each sentence with
two spaces, but in these days of word processors this is now less
common.) A 12-point font such as Courier, Times New Roman or
Arial should be used throughout. In Scrivener, however, you can
write using any font and formatting you choose--the options in
compile can be used to change the formatting in the compiled
document. In the &quot;Novel&quot; project template, the compile settings
will deal with changing the font and formatting to those required
for standard manuscript format.

         Top, bottom, left and right margins should all be
approximately one inch, not allowing for the page header. Each
page except for the title page should include a header comprising
the author's real surname, the title of the work (or a key word
from the title) in capitals, and the page number. In Scrivener,
all of this is handled for you by the &quot;Manuscript&quot; compile
formats.

                                                          #
         Scene breaks are indicated with the hash character. Do not
simply add an extra line space as this can be missed by the
typesetter. The &quot;Separators&quot; tab of Scrivener's compile format
designer can handle this for you if you are writing each scene as
a separate text document. In general, this will be handled by
compile in Scrivener, however, so that you can either leave empty
lines in the text (and Scrivener will insert the hash character


                                                            Nonymous / MANUSCRIPT FORMAT / 3

during compile) or use different documents for different sections
(and Scrivener will add the hash between them).

         Some editors and publishers prefer emphasis to be indicated
by underlining rather than italics. In Scrivener, this conversion
can be handled by compile. The &quot;Manuscript (Courier)&quot; compile
format will convert italics to underlines, whereas the
&quot;Manuscript (Times)&quot; format will not.


                                                            Nonymous / MANUSCRIPT FORMAT / 4

                                                 Chapter Two

         Begin each new chapter on a new page like this with the
chapter number about a third of the way down the page. In
Scrivener, this can all be dealt with in the compile settings. In
Scrivener, all of this is handled automatically by the
&quot;Manuscript&quot; compile formats.

         Although it is often said that writers should not use the
word-count features of their computers to determine the number of
words in a manuscript, most publishers and editors will happily
accept a word processor word count these days. In Scrivener, you
can insert a word count of your manuscript by using Edit &gt; Insert
&gt; Draft Word Count (for a manuscript, you may wish to have the
word count rounded to the nearest 100, in which case select Edit
&gt; Insert &gt; Draft Word Count &gt; Rounded To Nearest 100--this
feature is used on the title page of this manuscript, in fact).

         Note that the actual word count will not be inserted, but a
tag that will be substituted for the word count upon export. In


                                                            Nonymous / MANUSCRIPT FORMAT / 5

reality, the formatting of a manuscript is often only really
crucial when submitting directly to a publisher--first time
novelists will usually submit to an agent first, and agents are
often less picky about format and will point you in the right
direction before submitting your work to a publisher.

                                                          #
         Much of the information contained in this document is based
on a description of novel manuscript format by Matt Carless for
the BBC Writers' Room website: http://www.bbc.co.uk/writersroom

                                                  # # # # #
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="84360D9A-E937-4AE6-BB3E-7A49C0CC6B71">
            <Title>scene</Title>
            <Text>Wendy,  Quinton and David made there way through the forest. Each caring there backpacks full of camping gear. It would be dark soon and they hoped to make camp before then. 
How did you say you found this place Wendy?
I told you David, I was walking Butters when she ran into the woods chasing a squirrel or a bird or who know what.
You need to get that dog train.
Thank you Quinton, Wendy said sarcastically
Anyway Butters took off and I ran after her until I got to this open field next to a river.
It’s the perfect place to film in unexplored territory. That was the problem with going to film school in a small town. Nature shoots are beautiful. Everyone filmed at the same locations. 
This is gonna be great spending time in nature. Nothing like peace and quite to get your creative juices flowing.










It had been about six months since she had gone out. She would have to shake the dust off he club clothes but it would be fun to go out dancing with her beastie.
OK I’ll go. 

Two and a half hours later Autumn and Violet walked into the club. 

Purple lights swirled around the first floor. Bodies swayed to the heavy bass of the music.  The DJ was on a platform the the left that looked large enough for a live band. To the right a long padded bench lined the wall with small a small table and chair placed ever four feet or so for additional seating.  Behind the long padded bench was the stairway to the second floor. Straight ahead was the bar.  
I know where I’m headed first. Autumn said nodding toward the bar.
Shots! Shots! Shots! Good idea Violet agreed as they both started to weave there way through the sea of people on the dance floor.

Two vodka shots later Autumn felt a tingle down her back as if someone had caressed her. She turned around looking for the asshole who tried to cop a feel but no one was there.  Still she felt something.
“Autumn?” Vi called of the music. You ok?
“Yeah” Autumn said toying with her necklace. I just… It’s nothing. Its just been a while since Ive been out.
Oh ok well lets go shake the rust off. Vi grabbed my had and we headed back to the floor.
Three bass throbbing, waist winding, hip shaking songs later I still couldn’t shake the tingle down my back. 
I need a drink. Water.
Ok. I’ll come with.
No. Its ok.  Im a big girl. Besides the cutie in the hat is checking you out. Have fun.
Autumn just needed a moment to shake off the weird feeling. Plus the dance floor was packed and she had to get some air.  Autumn had no idea where she was going she just had to get away from the crowd. Absent minded she walked toward seating area but continued past the padded bench to the staircase and headed for the club’s second floor balcony. The second floor provided a birds eye view of the dance floor below.  She could see Violet slow grinding on the Spurs hat cutie.  Autumn wished she could be so care free. Even now the tingle in her spine was spreading down her legs. And she was started to feel a bit light headed. Now was not the time for a panic attack. Autumn took one step backwards away from the rail and turn to head for a seat at the bar. Instead of a clear path Autumn hit a brick wall and stumbled backward against the rails. Her heels rolled and she braced for a humiliating fall. The crash never came. In a blink she was pulled forward and pressed against the brick wall again. But this time she felt a hand against her spine and the tingle started to spread through her body. 
 His dark wavy hair fell in front of his eyes like a curtain. But beyond that wavy veil were the most seductive pair of blue eyes starring at her.  His eye seemed to glow in the club lights. At second glance that wasnt seduction in those blue eyes it was anger.
Im so sorry. Im not feeling… Nevermind I’ll just get out of your way. 
The hand on Autumn spine pressed her even more firmly against the stranger before letting go.
Please. Im the one who should watch where theyre going. I apologize for my clumsiness. Let me buy you a drink. Im River.
Im Autumn but Im just drinking water and I need to head back to my friend, Autumn said gesturing toward the dance floor below. 
The young lady with the purple hair? I saw you dancing with her. Why don’t I buy a drink for both of you and I’ll walk you back to your friend to we can have a little time to talk.
Ok. That is very nice of you.
</Text>
        </Document>
